DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The  ppiiddffdd__ooppeenn()  system call creates a file descriptor that refers to
       the task referenced by _p_i_d.  The file descriptor  is  returned  as  the
       function result; the close‐on‐exec flag is set on the file descriptor.

       The  _f_l_a_g_s  argument  either has the value 0, or contains the following
       flags:

       PPIIDDFFDD__NNOONNBBLLOOCCKK (since Linux 5.10)
              Return a nonblocking file descriptor.  If the task  referred  to
              by  the  file descriptor has not yet terminated, then an attempt
              to wait on the file descriptor using wwaaiittiidd(2) will  immediately
              return the error EEAAGGAAIINN rather than blocking.

       PPIIDDFFDD__TTHHRREEAADD (since Linux 6.9)
              Create  a  file  descriptor  that  refers  to a specific thread,
              rather than a process (thread‐group leader).  If  this  flag  is
              not set, _p_i_d must refer to a process.

