NNOOTTEESS
       The following code sequence can be used to obtain a file descriptor for
       the child of ffoorrkk(2):

           pid = fork();
           if (pid > 0) {     /* If parent */
               pidfd = pidfd_open(pid, 0);
               ...
           }

       Even  if  the  child  has  already  terminated  by  the  time  of   the
       ppiiddffdd__ooppeenn() call, its PID will not have been recycled and the returned
       file descriptor will refer to the resulting zombie process.  Note, how‐
       ever,  that  this  is  guaranteed only if the following conditions hold
       true:

       •  the disposition of SSIIGGCCHHLLDD has not been explicitly  set  to  SSIIGG__IIGGNN
          (see ssiiggaaccttiioonn(2));

       •  the SSAA__NNOOCCLLDDWWAAIITT flag was not specified while establishing a handler
          for  SSIIGGCCHHLLDD  or  while  setting  the  disposition of that signal to
          SSIIGG__DDFFLL (see ssiiggaaccttiioonn(2)); and

       •  the zombie process was not reaped elsewhere in  the  program  (e.g.,
          either by an asynchronously executed signal handler or by wwaaiitt(2) or
          similar in another thread).

       If any of these conditions does not hold, then the child process (along
       with a PID file descriptor that refers to it) should instead be created
       using cclloonnee(2) with the CCLLOONNEE__PPIIDDFFDD flag.

   UUssee ccaasseess ffoorr PPIIDD ffiillee ddeessccrriippttoorrss
       A PID file descriptor returned by ppiiddffdd__ooppeenn() (or by cclloonnee(2) with the
       CCLLOONNEE__PPIIDD flag) can be used for the following purposes:

       •  The ppiiddffdd__sseenndd__ssiiggnnaall(2) system call can be used to send a signal to
          the process referred to by a PID file descriptor.

       •  A PID file descriptor can be monitored using ppoollll(2), sseelleecctt(2), and
          eeppoollll(7).

          When  the  task  that  it refers to terminates and becomes a zombie,
          these interfaces indicate the file descriptor as readable (EEPPOOLLLLIINN).
          When the task is reaped, these interfaces  produce  a  hangup  event
          (EEPPOOLLLLHHUUPP).

          Note,  however,  that  in the current implementation, nothing can be
          read from the file descriptor (rreeaadd(2) on the file descriptor  fails
          with  the  error  EEIINNVVAALL).   The polling behavior depends on whether
          PPIIDDFFDD__TTHHRREEAADD flag was used when obtaining the file descriptor:

          •  With PPIIDDFFDD__TTHHRREEAADD, the file descriptor becomes readable when  the
             task  exits and becomes a zombie, even if the thread‐group is not
             empty.

          •  Without PPIIDDFFDD__TTHHRREEAADD, the file descriptor becomes  readable  only
             when the last thread in the thread group exits.

       •  If the PID file descriptor refers to a child of the calling process,
          then it can be waited on using wwaaiittiidd(2).

       •  The  ppiiddffdd__ggeettffdd(2) system call can be used to obtain a duplicate of
          a file descriptor of another process referred to by a PID  file  de‐
          scriptor.

       •  A PID file descriptor can be used as the argument of sseettnnss(2) in or‐
          der  to  move into one or more of the same namespaces as the process
          referred to by the file descriptor.

       •  A PID file descriptor can be used as the  argument  of  pprroocceessss__mmaadd‐‐
          vviissee(2)  in  order to provide advice on the memory usage patterns of
          the process referred to by the file descriptor.

       The ppiiddffdd__ooppeenn() system call is the preferred way of  obtaining  a  PID
       file descriptor for an already existing process.  The alternative is to
       obtain  a  file  descriptor by opening a _/_p_r_o_c_/pid directory.  However,
       the latter technique is possible only  if  the  pprroocc(5)  filesystem  is
       mounted;  furthermore,  the file descriptor obtained in this way is _n_o_t
       pollable and can’t be waited on with wwaaiittiidd(2).

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
       The program below opens a PID file descriptor for the process whose PID
       is specified as its command‐line argument.  It  then  uses  ppoollll(2)  to
       monitor  the  file  descriptor  for  process  exit,  as indicated by an
       EEPPOOLLLLIINN event.

   PPrrooggrraamm ssoouurrccee

       #define _GNU_SOURCE
       #include <poll.h>
       #include <stdio.h>
       #include <stdlib.h>
       #include <sys/syscall.h>
       #include <sys/types.h>
       #include <unistd.h>

       static int
       pidfd_open(pid_t pid, unsigned int flags)
       {
           return syscall(SYS_pidfd_open, pid, flags);
       }

       int
       main(int argc, char *argv[])
       {
           int            pidfd, ready;
           struct pollfd  pollfd;

           if (argc != 2) {
               fprintf(stderr, "Usage: %s <pid>\n", argv[0]);
               exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);
           }

           pidfd = pidfd_open(atoi(argv[1]), 0);
           if (pidfd == -1) {
               perror("pidfd_open");
               exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
           }

           pollfd.fd = pidfd;
           pollfd.events = POLLIN;

           ready = poll(&pollfd, 1, -1);
           if (ready == -1) {
               perror("poll");
               exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
           }

           printf("Events (%#x): POLLIN is %sset\n", pollfd.revents,
                  (pollfd.revents & POLLIN) ? "" : "not ");

           close(pidfd);
           exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);
       }

